.TH TAG 1 "September 2026" "finder-tags 6.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tag \- manipulate macOS Finder tags while preserving tag order
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tag
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ path ...]
.br
.B tag --add
.I TAGS
.RI [ placement ]
.I path ...
.br
.B tag --remove
.I TAGS path ...
.br
.B tag --set
.I TAGS path ...
.br
.B tag --match
.I TAGS
.RI [ path ...]
.br
.B tag --usage
.I TAGS
.RI [ path ...]
.br
.B tag --find
.I TAGS
.RI [ path ...]
.br
.B tag --copy
.I SOURCE DESTINATION
.br
.B tag --move
.I TAG POSITION path ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B finder-tags
installs an executable named
.B tag
for listing and modifying Finder tags. Stored tag order is preserved by default.
The interface is compatible with jdberry/tag where practical.
.SH OPERATIONS
.TP
.BR -l , " --list"
List tags. This is the default operation.
.TP
.BR -a , " --add " TAGS
Add or re-case tags without disturbing unrelated tag order.
.TP
.B --append TAGS
Alias for --add with last/right/top placement.
.TP
.B --prepend TAGS
Alias for --add with first/left/bottom placement.
.TP
.BR -r , " --remove " TAGS
Remove matching tags. A TAGS value of * removes all tags.
.TP
.BR -s , " --set " TAGS
Replace the complete tag array.
.TP
.B --copy SOURCE DESTINATION
Replace DESTINATION's tags with SOURCE's tags.
.TP
.BR -m , " --match " TAGS
List traversed files containing all requested tags.
.TP
.BR -u , " --usage " TAGS
Count all tags on traversed files matching TAGS.
.TP
.BR -f , " --find " TAGS
Use Spotlight to find matching files, optionally scoped to supplied paths.
.TP
.B --move TAG POSITION
Move one tag to a new position.
.SH ORDERING
.TP
.B --at POSITION
Insertion position for --add or --move. POSITION may be a zero-based index,
first/left/bottom, or last/right/top.
.TP
.B --before TAG
Place added or moved tags immediately before TAG.
.TP
.B --after TAG
Place added or moved tags immediately after TAG.
.TP
.B --sorted-tags
Sort tags for display. Mutating operations also sort the final stored array.
Read-only operations never write merely because this option is present.
.TP
.BR -V , " --reverse"
Reverse display order only.
.TP
.BR -C , " --case-sensitive"
Use exact-case matching instead of the default case-insensitive matching.
.SH OUTPUT
.TP
.BR -c , " --color"
Display known Finder tag colors when stdout is a terminal.
.TP
.BR -n , " --filename"
Show filenames. --name is an alias.
.TP
.BR -N , " --no-filename"
Hide filenames. --no-name is an alias.
.TP
.BR -t , " --tags"
Show tags.
.TP
.BR -T , " --no-tags"
Hide tags.
.TP
.BR -g , " --one-per-line"
Display each tag on its own line. --garrulous is an alias.
.TP
.BR -G , " --comma-separated"
Display comma-separated tags. --no-garrulous is an alias.
.TP
.BR -p , " --slash"
Append / to directory names.
.TP
.BR -0 , " --null"
Terminate text records with NUL. --nul is an alias.
.TP
.B --absolute
Display absolute logical paths.
.TP
.B --jsonl
Emit newline-delimited JSON. --ndjson is an alias.
.SH PATH INPUT AND TRAVERSAL
.TP
.B --stdin
Read newline-delimited paths from standard input.
.TP
.B --stdin0
Read NUL-delimited paths from standard input.
.TP
.BR -A , " --all"
Include hidden files while enumerating.
.TP
.BR -e , " --enter"
Enumerate immediate contents of explicit directories.
.TP
.BR -R , " -d ," " --recursive"
Recursively enumerate directories.
.TP
.B --no-follow-symlinks
Do not recursively follow symlinked directories or explicitly resolve paths
before Foundation tag I/O.
.TP
.B --follow-symlinks
Restore default symlink following.
.SH SAFETY
.TP
.B --dry-run
Compute and display intended mutations without writing. --dryrun is an alias.
.SH TAG SYNTAX
TAGS is comma separated. Quoted CSV-style components permit literal commas,
for example:
.PP
.nf
tag --set 'Red,"Project, Alpha","Needs review"' file
.fi
.PP
Matching is case-insensitive by default while stored spelling is preserved.
Tag names containing CR, LF, or NUL are rejected because Foundation does not
reliably round-trip them as Finder tags.
.SH FILES
See the finder-tags README and REVIEW.md for detailed behavior, reliability
notes, compatibility differences, and JSONL fields.
